<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Early Dynastic Egypt</h1><p class="st-meta">3100 BCE – 2686 BCE (approx.) · capital Memphis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The First and Second Dynasties, which turned a newly united Nile valley into the kingdom of the pharaohs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was united around 3100 BCE when the Thinite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a>, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hor-aha">Hor-Aha</a> (often identified with the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menes">Menes</a>), brought the Delta under the southern kingship. A new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt">Memphis</a>, was founded where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley meets the Delta, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> began, succeeding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/proto-kingdom-of-thinis">Proto-kingdom of Thinis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings of the First and Second Dynasties created the lasting institutions of pharaonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>: a court and treasury, taxation recorded in hieroglyphs, the double crown and the royal titulary. Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> kings were buried at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a> with sacrificed retainers, while officials built great mastaba tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>. Den first used the title 'King of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>', and expeditions reached Sinai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and stone.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-dynasty-of-egypt">Second Dynasty</a> saw unrest, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peribsen">Peribsen</a> adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> as his patron, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khasekhemwy">Khasekhemwy</a> reunited the country. His successors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-egypt">Third Dynasty</a>, from around 2686 BCE, opened the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt</a> and its age of pyramid building.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/early-dynastic-egypt.jpg" alt="Early Dynastic Egypt" width="320" height="454"><figcaption>Image: पाटलिपुत्र · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chronology_of_state_formation_in_Ancient_Egypt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>3100 BCE – 2686 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd>32nd century BCE – <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Memphis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Narmer, Den, Khasekhemwy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Egyptian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Egyptian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>First and Second Dynasties, Archaic Egypt, Thinite Period</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2893&amp;focus=early-dynastic-egypt" aria-label="Early Dynastic Egypt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5493 1088 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Early Dynastic Egypt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5813 1344L5842 1467M5813 1344L5794 1297M5813 1344L5847 1613M5813 1344L5813 1344"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5842 1467h0M5794 1297h0M5847 1613h0M5813 1344h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5813 1344h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5813 1344h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Memphis. <a href="/atlas?year=-2893&amp;focus=early-dynastic-egypt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
